    {
      "id": "2h_2DzTnU3XvouXjLM4aE",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 783.7053000563313,
      "y": 2519.0844694860216,
      "width": 571.4968842947172,
      "height": 1275,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aG",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1223894989,
      "version": 3799,
      "versionNonce": 2097166467,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1779628782262,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "ok discovered a massive bug, or two actually then \nboth seen in the above example... the problems might\nbe related tho. in fact i think theyre directly linked\nbasically the l shift shouldnt add to bank even if its in\nfreqs, as long as the quota is still met within the window.\nthis stems directly from the fact that im forcing 0 for\neverything in freqs, then removing, but that doesnt\naccount for the fact that there could be multiple\noccurences in a window, so we're undercouting\nas a result, not only do we miss zaxy, we also miss xyz\noh wait but thats actually unrelated now that i realize. rn\nmy logic would jump straight to l=5(z) after finding zaxy\nbut it should actually keep shifting l. so this whole thing\nis flawed. fuck.\nok wait i thought of a solution to the second problem:\nwhen l shift causes an invalid window, instead of jumping,\nu simply do nothing (not even increment l) and let r shift\nand find a valid window. then r will naturally catch up\ntrying to minimize this valid window, and repeat. so that\nshould be good. we need to fix the first problem and\nrethink how to decide validity of a window in O(1) time\ntho. well hold on, if  its just english letters we could do\nit in O(52n) no? if we do \"valid if all of hashmap is zero\"?\nbetter yet we just compare the two hashmaps and do\nthat approach... cause actaully thats O(52) not O(n),\nnow that i think about it \nbut hold on it's not that simple because we can't just cap\nthe counts because again that's the same issue. We need\nthe bank to be accurate. We need it to actually you know\nthe frequencies to actually like represent how often they\ncome up in the string or in t so in that case, we need to\nrethink how we checked the validity of a certain window\nand I don't want to just do a simple scan and compare of\nthe two hash maps. I feel like we could do a helper\nfunction for that and then put that in the while loop but\nI don't like that solution. It feels weird. It feels like not\nthe standard and it's just an efficient, but I think it will\nstill work because I do think it's still like O(n)\nyo wait what if you keep track of one specific number\ncalled like a score or something and then you only\nincrement the score if for the specific letter you are\nincrementing in the frequency map that number that new\nnumber that it goes to is smaller than equal to it it's\noriginal number in the original hash map\nThen you keep letting it freely you know move up,\nhowever you want, but if for the specific thing it's\nfrequency is already greater than or equal to the\nfrequency in freqs, you don't increment the score\nThen when the score is exactly equal to the score that\nyou're supposed to have the window is valid\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "ok discovered a massive bug, or two actually then \nboth seen in the above example... the problems might\nbe related tho. in fact i think theyre directly linked\nbasically the l shift shouldnt add to bank even if its in freqs, as long as the quota is still met within the window.\nthis stems directly from the fact that im forcing 0 for everything in freqs, then removing, but that doesnt account for the fact that there could be multiple occurences in a window, so we're undercouting\nas a result, not only do we miss zaxy, we also miss xyz\noh wait but thats actually unrelated now that i realize. rn my logic would jump straight to l=5(z) after finding zaxy but it should actually keep shifting l. so this whole thing is flawed. fuck.\nok wait i thought of a solution to the second problem: when l shift causes an invalid window, instead of jumping, u simply do nothing (not even increment l) and let r shift and find a valid window. then r will naturally catch up trying to minimize this valid window, and repeat. so that should be good. we need to fix the first problem and rethink how to decide validity of a window in O(1) time tho. well hold on, if  its just english letters we could do it in O(52n) no? if we do \"valid if all of hashmap is zero\"? better yet we just compare the two hashmaps and do that approach... cause actaully thats O(52) not O(n), now that i think about it \nbut hold on it's not that simple because we can't just cap the counts because again that's the same issue. We need the bank to be accurate. We need it to actually you know the frequencies to actually like represent how often they come up in the string or in t so in that case, we need to rethink how we checked the validity of a certain window and I don't want to just do a simple scan and compare of the two hash maps. I feel like we could do a helper function for that and then put that in the while loop but I don't like that solution. It feels weird. It feels like not the standard and it's just an efficient, but I think it will still work because I do think it's still like O(n)\nyo wait what if you keep track of one specific number called like a score or something and then you only increment the score if for the specific letter you are incrementing in the frequency map that number that new number that it goes to is smaller than equal to it it's original number in the original hash map\nThen you keep letting it freely you know move up, however you want, but if for the specific thing it's frequency is already greater than or equal to the frequency in freqs, you don't increment the score\nThen when the score is exactly equal to the score that you're supposed to have the window is valid\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
